##
    One name, several versions.

    Functions sharing a name are told apart by what they take. Which version a call means is
    settled while it is checked, from the types of the arguments written — never at the moment
    it runs, and never from what a value happens to hold.

    The order is: how many arguments, then an exact match, then what the arguments can widen to.
    Anything that gets as far as two versions fitting equally well is reported rather than
    guessed at, and samples/negatives/compile/overloads.pc holds those.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.CountingFirst();
        Program.ExactBeatsWidening();
        Program.WideningWhenNothingIsExact();
        Program.TheNearestModelWins();
        Program.AnOptionalIsItsOwnType();
        Program.AcrossAParentAndItsChild();
        Program.AnOverrideIsNotASecondVersion();
    end function

    # ---- How many, before what kind -----------------------------------------------

    ##
        The count is asked first, so two versions taking different numbers of arguments never
        compete. This is what makes an overload set easy to read: most of the time the count
        alone says which one is meant.
    ##
    function CountingFirst()
        Console.WriteLine("one:  " + Program.Join("a"));
        Console.WriteLine("two:  " + Program.Join("a", "b"));
        Console.WriteLine("three:" + Program.Join("a", "b", "c"));
    end function

    string function Join(string a)
        yield a;
    end function

    string function Join(string a, string b)
        yield a + b;
    end function

    string function Join(string a, string b, string c)
        yield a + b + c;
    end function

    # ---- Exact before widening ----------------------------------------------------

    ##
        An integer widens to a real and to a fraction, so all three versions below could accept
        one. The exact version wins outright: a value keeps the type it was written as rather
        than the first type it fits.

        This is the rule worth knowing, because it is what stops adding a version taking a real
        from quietly changing what every existing call to the integer one does.
    ##
    function ExactBeatsWidening()
        Console.WriteLine("integer:  " + Program.Describe(3));
        Console.WriteLine("real:     " + Program.Describe(3.5));
        Console.WriteLine("fraction: " + Program.Describe(1|2));
    end function

    string function Describe(integer n)
        yield "an integer, " + n;
    end function

    string function Describe(real r)
        yield "a real, " + r;
    end function

    string function Describe(fraction f)
        yield "a fraction, " + f;
    end function

    # ---- Widening, where nothing is exact -----------------------------------------

    ##
        With no version taking an integer, the one taking a real accepts it — an integer is a
        real. Nothing is lost on the way, which is why it happens without being asked for.

        A character is not a small integer, so it reaches the version taking a string only if
        one is written; here it reaches its own.
    ##
    function WideningWhenNothingIsExact()
        Console.WriteLine("widened: " + Program.Measure(4));
        Console.WriteLine("exact:   " + Program.Measure(4.0));
        Console.WriteLine("letter:  " + Program.Spell('q'));
        Console.WriteLine("word:    " + Program.Spell("quiet"));
    end function

    string function Measure(real r)
        yield "read as a real, " + r;
    end function

    string function Spell(character c)
        yield "one character, " + c;
    end function

    string function Spell(string s)
        yield "a string of " + s.Count;
    end function

    # ---- The nearest model wins ---------------------------------------------------

    ##
        A version taking the exact model beats one taking a model it descends from. What decides
        is the type the argument was written as, not what the value turned out to be — so an
        Animal holding a Dog reaches the Animal version, and the Dog reaches the Dog version.

        That is the opposite of how an override behaves, and the difference is worth holding on
        to: an override is chosen by the value, an overload by the type.
    ##
    function TheNearestModelWins()
        Dog rex = new Dog();
        Animal asAnimal = rex;

        Console.WriteLine("as a dog:    " + Program.Feed(rex));
        Console.WriteLine("as an animal:" + Program.Feed(asAnimal));
    end function

    string function Feed(Animal a)
        yield "something an animal eats";
    end function

    string function Feed(Dog d)
        yield "something a dog eats";
    end function

    # ---- An optional is its own type ----------------------------------------------

    ##
        'integer' and 'integer?' are different types, so a version taking each is two versions
        and neither is a widening of the other. A value that might be absent reaches the one
        that says so.
    ##
    function AnOptionalIsItsOwnType()
        integer? missing = Program.Nothing();

        Console.WriteLine("present: " + Program.Count(7));
        Console.WriteLine("absent:  " + Program.Count(missing));
    end function

    string function Count(integer n)
        yield "a number, " + n;
    end function

    string function Count(integer? n)
        yield "maybe a number, " + if n.HasValue() then "yes" else "no";
    end function

    integer? function Nothing()
        integer? none;
        yield none;
    end function

    # ---- Across a parent and its child ---------------------------------------------

    ##
        A child adding a version does not take away the ones its parent wrote. Both are versions
        of the same name, and a call reaches whichever fits — so 'Which(1)' below runs the
        parent's and 'Which("x")' runs the child's, through the same variable.

        Written the other way round it would mean that adding a function to a child could stop
        code compiling that has nothing to do with it.
    ##
    function AcrossAParentAndItsChild()
        Ledger ledger = new Ledger();

        Console.WriteLine("parent's: " + ledger.Which(1));
        Console.WriteLine("child's:  " + ledger.Which("x"));
    end function

    # ---- An override is not a second version ---------------------------------------

    ##
        An override and the function it overrides take the same things, so they are one version
        rather than two — otherwise every call to an overridden function would be a tie between
        the two of them. Which body runs is then decided by what the value turned out to be,
        which is what 'virtual' is for.
    ##
    function AnOverrideIsNotASecondVersion()
        Animal pet = new Dog();

        Console.WriteLine("said:  " + pet.Speaks());

        # Named rather than called, and bound to the same body a call would have reached.
        string delegate() heard = pet.Speaks;

        Console.WriteLine("heard: " + heard());
    end function

end model

# @summary: An animal, for showing an overload chosen by the type a value is held under.
model Animal
    # @summary: What this animal says.
    # @yields: nothing in particular, for an animal of no particular kind.
    public virtual string function Speaks()
        yield "...";
    end function
end model

# @summary: An animal that says something in particular.
model Dog extends Animal
    # @summary: What a dog says.
    # @yields: "woof".
    public override string function Speaks()
        yield "woof";
    end function
end model

# @summary: A parent holding one version of a name.
model Record
    # @summary: Answers to a number.
    # @n: the number.
    # @yields: which version ran.
    public string function Which(integer n)
        yield "the parent's, given " + n;
    end function
end model

##
    @summary: A child adding a version of the same name.

    @remarks: The parent's version is still reachable through a Ledger, which is the whole point
    of the section that uses this.
##
model Ledger extends Record
    # @summary: Answers to a word.
    # @s: the word.
    # @yields: which version ran.
    public string function Which(string s)
        yield "the child's, given " + s;
    end function
end model
